We immediately notice a call to gets, which is vulnerable to a classic stack-based buffer overflow.
After the gets call, the program performs a check on the saved return address. It masks the value with 0xb0000000 and exits if the result matches this value. This effectively prevents us from overwriting the return address with a stack address (which typically starts with 0xb.......).
However, at the end of the function, the program calls strdup on our input and returns normally.
This means that if we overwrite the saved return address with the address returned by strdup, we can redirect execution to heap memory, which passes the address check.
